// Recipe content (ARCHITECTURE.md §3.6) — data, never hardcoded in sim_core.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader must reject or migrate a mismatched version rather than
// silently misreading a changed shape.
//
// Milestone 4 (First full chain) turns this into a real multi-step
// production line: `sim_core::sim::World` builds one belt+machine stage per
// entry below, IN THIS FILE'S ORDER, chaining each stage's output into the
// next stage's input (see sim_core/src/sim/world.rs's module doc). That is
// what makes adding a processing step a pure data-file edit — appending a
// recipe here, whose `inputs` names an item the previous recipe's `outputs`
// produces, adds a pipeline stage with no Rust changes at all.
//
// Milestone 6 (Fluids) bumps `schema_version` to 2 and adds the optional
// `fluid_inputs` field: (fluid id, whole units) consumed per craft from the
// machine's connected pipe network (ARCHITECTURE.md §3.4). It defaults to
// empty, so every recipe that doesn't name it is unchanged — but v1 files
// are still *rejected*, not migrated; see `CURRENT_SCHEMA_VERSION`'s doc in
// sim_core/src/sim/recipe.rs for why. `World` builds its single fixed pipe
// run for whichever recipe below declares `fluid_inputs`, carrying whatever
// fluid that recipe names — so which stage is plumbed is a property of this
// file, not of any Rust code.
//
// The chain below is a simplified stand-in for GAMEPLAY.md §2's tier 1/2
// tomato-sauce/ketchup progression (real ketchup also needs sugar and spices
// as separate raw ingredients, and GAMEPLAY.md §4 calls out its bottling step
// as part of the same equipment tier) — this file keeps every recipe past
// the first to a single *solid* input/single output so `World`'s
// one-straight-line assumption holds; multi-ingredient convergence on the
// belt side is a later milestone's branching-topology concern, not this
// one's. (A fluid input is not belt-borne, so it adds an ingredient without
// touching that topology at all — which is exactly why milestone 6 could
// add one to an existing stage instead of inventing a new one.) Don't read
// this as the final ketchup recipe.
//
// THE FRONT END IS THREE REAL MACHINES (the "Machines improvement"
// milestone's ticket 2). It used to be two abstract steps — a "cooker"
// turning tomatoes straight into tomato sauce, then a "reducer" turning that
// into ketchup — which described a process nobody would recognize from a
// real plant. It is now the three steps a ketchup line actually has: crush,
// mix, cook. That is a pure content change: the chain is still one straight
// line, still one stage per entry in file order, and `sim_core` needed no
// code change to grow from four stages to five. The downstream
// bottling/boxing stages below are untouched.
//
// PROCESS TIMES follow the work each machine does, so the line has a visible
// bottleneck rather than five interchangeable stages: crushing is mechanical
// and quick (60 ticks = 1s at ARCHITECTURE.md §3.9's 60 ticks/sec), blending
// a little slower (90), and the cook-down is by far the longest step (240) —
// which is precisely why the cooking vat is also the biggest machine on the
// line (`data/machines.ron`).
(
    schema_version: 2,
    recipes: [
        // Crush: whole tomatoes in, paste out. The chain's raw-ingredient
        // entry point, so this is the one recipe consuming an item nothing
        // else produces.
        Recipe(
            id: "tomato_paste",
            inputs: [("tomato", 4)],
            outputs: [("tomato_paste", 1)],
            machine: "crusher",
            time_ticks: 60,
        ),
        // Mix: paste plus vinegar makes an uncooked ketchup mix. This is
        // milestone 6's fluid-requiring recipe, and mixing is where it
        // belongs — GAMEPLAY.md §2's tier table has vinegar entering at
        // ketchup ("+ vinegar, sugar, spices"), and a real line adds it in
        // the blend, not in the cook. `World` builds its one pipe run for
        // whichever recipe in this file declares `fluid_inputs`, so moving
        // the fluid requirement from the old `ketchup` recipe to this one
        // re-plumbed the pipe run with no Rust change at all — that is the
        // data-driven property working, not a coincidence.
        Recipe(
            id: "ketchup_mix",
            inputs: [("tomato_paste", 1)],
            fluid_inputs: [("vinegar", 2)],
            outputs: [("ketchup_mix", 1)],
            machine: "mixer",
            time_ticks: 90,
        ),
        // Cook: the long batch reduction that turns the mix into finished
        // ketchup. The slowest step in the chain and the reason the cooking
        // vat is a 2x2 machine.
        Recipe(
            id: "ketchup",
            inputs: [("ketchup_mix", 1)],
            outputs: [("ketchup", 1)],
            machine: "cooking_vat",
            time_ticks: 240,
        ),
        Recipe(
            id: "bottled_ketchup",
            inputs: [("ketchup", 1)],
            outputs: [("bottled_ketchup", 1)],
            machine: "bottler",
            time_ticks: 60,
        ),
        // Added AFTER the 3-stage chain above was already working end to
        // end, purely by editing this file — no sim_core/src or
        // godot_project/*.gd changes accompany this entry. This is
        // milestone 4's required proof that extending the chain is a
        // data-file-only change: `World::new` (sim_core/src/sim/world.rs)
        // builds one stage per recipe here, in order, with no recipe id
        // baked into Rust anywhere.
        Recipe(
            id: "boxed_ketchup",
            inputs: [("bottled_ketchup", 1)],
            outputs: [("boxed_ketchup", 1)],
            machine: "packer",
            time_ticks: 40,
        ),
    ],
)
